															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/trinitite" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1375648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1375648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973924" alt="Trinitite" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1375648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/trinitite" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1375648); return false;" >Trinitite</a></span> <span class="odate time_1340407482 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Jun 2012 23:24</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1487507">
									

<p>I was wondering about this myself and here's my theory: they are in some ways advanced but are in other ways weak, and would like to take over Earth's biosphere (and maybe manufacturing capabilities?) intact as a quick way of building up their strength. Animorphs had this: the evil aliens (who also wanted to take humans as host bodies, but that's beside the point here) had escaped from their homeworld where they had been held under blockade by the slightly better aliens. They were low in number but had a reasonably high technology base. They wanted to relieve their homeworld with a vast force in the future by growing in numbers and power on Earth. They couldn't do a full invasion and didn't have a lot of ships or space superfactories, but did have enough manpower to be able to fight when they revealed themselves, and what weapons they did have were devastating-though they could be destroyed, each was able to take on a lot of soldiers and aircraft simultaneously. Basically, they wanted to slowly take Earth over broadly intact and undamaged, by slow infiltration, and IIRC they did try to use human factories to nefarious purposes as well.</p>

							</div>

								
